  "title": {
    "en": "Hachiōji Minamino: a 1997 new town on very old hills",
    "ja": "八王子みなみ野 — 古い丘陵に生まれた1997年のニュータウン"
  },
  "description": {
    "en": "How a stretch of the Tama Hills south of Hachiōji became Minamino City, and what was there long before the station opened.",
    "ja": "八王子の南、多摩丘陵の一角がどのように「みなみ野シティ」になったのか。駅ができるずっと前、そこに何があったのか。"
  },
  "content": {
    "en": {
      "root": {
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "version": 1,
        "type": "root",
        "children": [
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Hachiōji Minamino City (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "674f54691aab5cab",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "八王子みなみ野シティ",
                  "r": "Hachiōji Minamino Shiti",
                  "en": "Hachiōji Minamino City"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": ") is one of the youngest neighbourhoods in Hachiōji. It sits about 40 km west of central Tokyo, two to five kilometres south of Hachiōji's centre, on the northern edge of the Tama Hills (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "0d635ad16ff68cfb",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "多摩丘陵",
                  "r": "Tama kyūryō",
                  "en": "the Tama Hills"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "). The town officially opened in March 1997. The hills it was built on have a much longer story.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "type": "heading",
            "tag": "h2",
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Before the new town",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Long before the housing went up, this was hill country, with woodland and paddies in the valleys. Two places nearby still show its older history.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 1,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Katakura Castle.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": " On the eastern tip of the Kohiki Hills, between the Yudono River and its tributary the Hyōe River, are the remains of Katakura Castle (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "9ac5cda56e4c572b",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "片倉城",
                  "r": "Katakura-jō",
                  "en": "Katakura Castle"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "). According to the usual account, the Yokoyama clan held the land from the middle of the Heian period. After the Wada Battle of 1213 it passed to Ōe no Hiromoto. The castle itself is a Muromachi-period hill castle. Who built it is uncertain: some accounts name Ōe Moroakira, others the Nagai family, retainers of the Ōgigayatsu Uesugi. It probably served the Later Hōjō as a fort, and it is said to have fallen with Hachiōji Castle in 1590. Hachiōji City turned the site into Katakura Castle Ruins Park in 1971, and Tokyo designated it a historic site on 3 March 1999. The dry moats and earthworks are still there. In spring the park is known for its wild dogtooth violets (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "b45f39493e1c3100",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "カタクリ",
                  "r": "katakuri",
                  "en": "dogtooth violet"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": ").",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "type": "block",
            "version": 2,
            "format": "",
            "fields": {
              "id": "mm-moat",
              "blockName": "",
              "blockType": "figure",
              "media": {
                "id": "minamino-moat",
                "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-moat-1600.webp",
                "filename": "minamino-moat-1600.webp",
                "mimeType": "image/webp",
                "width": 1600,
                "height": 1200,
                "alt": {
                  "en": "A grassy hollow between wooded banks in spring: the dry moat at Katakura Castle Ruins Park",
                  "ja": "春の木立にはさまれた、草に覆われたくぼ地。片倉城址公園の空堀"
                },
                "credit": "Photo: Takaosan fukujuso, CC BY-SA 4.0 (Wikimedia Commons)",
                "sizes": {
                  "480": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-moat-480.webp",
                    "width": 480,
                    "height": 360
                  },
                  "960": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-moat-960.webp",
                    "width": 960,
                    "height": 720
                  },
                  "1600": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-moat-1600.webp",
                    "width": 1600,
                    "height": 1200
                  }
                }
              },
              "caption": "The dry moat at Katakura Castle Ruins Park.",
              "credit": "Photo: Takaosan fukujuso, CC BY-SA 4.0 (Wikimedia Commons)"
            }
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 1,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Nanakuni Pass.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": " One of Minamino City's districts, Nanakuni (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "cead5dfc9ffadae4",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "七国",
                  "r": "Nanakuni",
                  "en": "\"seven provinces\""
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "), takes its name from Nanakuni Pass (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "a6bfa8608a643eb6",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "七国峠",
                  "r": "Nanakuni-tōge",
                  "en": "Nanakuni Pass"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "), on a ridge of the southern Tama Hills. A stretch of the old Kamakura road (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "7fc8e245b6f56442",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "鎌倉古道",
                  "r": "Kamakura kodō",
                  "en": "old Kamakura road"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": ") runs through it, and it was once a route people walked every day.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "type": "heading",
            "tag": "h2",
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Silk and the railway",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Hachiōji grew rich on silk. In the Edo period its markets traded silk cloth and raw silk, and the town was called \"the mulberry capital\" (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "cffd80dfa3dc0911",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "桑都",
                  "r": "sōto",
                  "en": "mulberry capital"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "). After Yokohama opened to foreign trade, the road that carried raw silk from Hachiōji to the port later came to be called the Silk Road (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "45fbdeef342d8b95",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "絹の道",
                  "r": "kinu no michi",
                  "en": "Silk Road"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": ").",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The railway that runs through Minamino today was built for the same trade. The Yokohama Line began in 1908 as the private Yokohama Railway, and its purpose was to carry raw silk produced around Hachiōji and in the Kōshin region to the port of Yokohama. The government leased it in 1910.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "type": "heading",
            "tag": "h2",
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Building Minamino City",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The new town was built through the South Hachiōji land readjustment project (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "753fdc075c938c2d",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "南八王子土地区画整理事業",
                  "r": "Minami-Hachiōji tochi kukaku seiri jigyō",
                  "en": "South Hachiōji land readjustment project"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "). The Housing and Urban Development Corporation, now the Urban Renaissance Agency (UR), carried it out. It covers five districts: Minamino, Nanakuni, Utsunuki-machi, Hyōe and Nishi-Katakura. It was also part of the plan to make Hachiōji and Tachikawa business centres for the Tama area.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The idea was a town that lives with the landscape (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "c94685ef0ba525b1",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "環境共生都市",
                  "r": "kankyō kyōsei toshi",
                  "en": "a town that lives with its environment"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "). It keeps the satoyama woodland (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "00adf8d54afb7bfa",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "里山",
                  "r": "satoyama",
                  "en": "managed village woodland"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": ") and the waterways of the hills, and the slopes are gentle and step-free. Pavements are wide, and most utility lines run underground. Shops and homes sit in separate zones, and businesses and warehouses face National Route 16 on the east side.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The station opened on 1 April 1997, at the same time as the town. Hachiōji-Minamino Station is on the JR Yokohama Line, 4.0 km from Hachiōji. Before it opened it was going to be called Utsunuki (",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "inlineBlock",
                "version": 1,
                "format": "",
                "fields": {
                  "id": "276597f07460b8bc",
                  "blockName": "",
                  "blockType": "jp",
                  "t": "宇津貫",
                  "r": "Utsunuki",
                  "en": "the old place name"
                }
              },
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "), after the old place name. In fiscal 2024 an average of 17,187 people a day boarded there, and smart platform doors came into use in November 2025.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "type": "block",
            "version": 2,
            "format": "",
            "fields": {
              "id": "mm-station",
              "blockName": "",
              "blockType": "figure",
              "media": {
                "id": "minamino-station",
                "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-station-1600.webp",
                "filename": "minamino-station-1600.webp",
                "mimeType": "image/webp",
                "width": 1600,
                "height": 898,
                "alt": {
                  "en": "The arched roof of Hachiōji-Minamino Station and the buildings beside it, with a wooded hill rising behind",
                  "ja": "八王子みなみ野駅のアーチ状の屋根と隣の建物。背後に樹木に覆われた丘がせまっている"
                },
                "credit": "Photo: Souka Kinmei, CC0 (Wikimedia Commons)",
                "sizes": {
                  "480": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-station-480.webp",
                    "width": 480,
                    "height": 269
                  },
                  "960": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-station-960.webp",
                    "width": 960,
                    "height": 539
                  },
                  "1600": {
                    "url": "/images/minamino/minamino-station-1600.webp",
                    "width": 1600,
                    "height": 898
                  }
                }
              },
              "caption": "Hachiōji-Minamino Station, which opened with the town in 1997. A wooded hill rises behind the buildings.",
              "credit": "Photo: Souka Kinmei, CC0 (Wikimedia Commons)"
            }
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The shops arrived over the next decade: MiO Minamino in 2003, Fresupo Hachiōji Minamino in 2005 and Across Mall Hachiōji Minamino in 2006. Construction of the Hachiōji South Bypass along the town's northern edge began in 2008. Tokyo University of Technology and Nihon Kogakuin Hachiōji College, in neighbouring Katakura, run school buses from the station.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "type": "heading",
            "tag": "h2",
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Today",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "version": 1,
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "The town was planned for 28,000 people. At the end of March 2022 about 26,893 lived there, so it is close to full. Walk from the station toward Katakura Castle Ruins Park and you pass from 1990s streets with no overhead wires to earthworks some five centuries old.",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "type": "paragraph"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "version": 1,
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "direction": "ltr",
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 0,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Photo credits: ",
                "type": "text",
                "version": 1
              },
              {
